1. Kernel math structure
This is abstract, but structurally precise. We don’t need final physics yet, just the form.
Core inputs:
(Z): proton number
(N): neutron number
(\vec{E}): environment vector (T, P, fields, density, etc.)
(\vec{S}): symmetry/constraint parameters
Core kernel function:
[ K(Z, N, \vec{E}, \vec{S}) \rightarrow { S_{\text{stab}}, E_b, \mathcal{D}, \mathcal{F} } ]
Where:
(S_{\text{stab}}): stability score (\in [0,1])
(E_b): binding energy per nucleon
(\mathcal{D}): set of allowed decay modes (alpha, beta, fission, etc.)
(\mathcal{F}): forbidden/allowed state flags (veto surfaces, magic numbers, etc.)
Internal structure (conceptual):
Shell term:
[ E_{\text{shell}}(Z,N) ]
Surface/volume term:
[ E_{\text{sv}}(Z,N) ]
Coulomb term:
[ E_{\text{C}}(Z) ]
Asymmetry term:
[ E_{\text{asym}}(Z,N) ]
Environment coupling:
[ E_{\text{env}}(\vec{E}) ]
Then:
[ E_b = f(E_{\text{shell}}, E_{\text{sv}}, E_{\text{C}}, E_{\text{asym}}, E_{\text{env}}) ]
[ S_{\text{stab}} = g(E_b, Z, N, \vec{E}, \vec{S}) ]
(\mathcal{D}) and (\mathcal{F}) are derived from thresholds and invariants inside (g).
The key: K is small, pure, and invariant—the rest of the engine orbits this.

